@@ -1,4 +1,23 @@
 Module lowwater "Low Water" {
   // The Bowl as a country beyond a march: the company that comes over the Rim is the world's own.
   Entity party: group "Your company".
+  // The Founders' Exchange, when the world holds a factor of it in the country behind the Rim: its carter's lots go
+  // south to the Collegium, which pays double for bronze that rings. A lot of bell-teeth in the carter's keeping,
+  // once the carter has gone (the Exchange says so in the world, not here), is in Halm's by the time the company
+  // has crossed the Rim, to be shown, rung, and wondered at. It is the same lot, from the same wall; it is not made
+  // here. If the company has bought the lot back or the carter never went, the tray is empty.
+  Entity carter: group "The carter of the Founders' Exchange.".
+  Place south_road "The south road, past the Exchange's yard" is room.
+  Rule halm_buys_lot when keeps(carter, ?t) and itemKind(?t, bell_teeth) and at(carter, south_road) {
+    Retract keeps(carter, ?t). Assert keeps(halm, ?t). Assert fromExchange(?t).
+  }
+  Relation fromExchange(item).
+  // What the company finds at the dig when the Exchange's lot has come: a thing on a cloth, and Halm's account of it. The
+  // lot is only here if the carter brought it; nothing else writes this.
+  Relation exchangeLot().
+  Rule exchange_lot_noted when keeps(halm, ?t) and fromExchange(?t) and not exchangeLot() { Assert exchangeLot(). }
+  Observe halm_tray at dig when exchangeLot() "Apart from the day's finds, on a clean cloth, lie twelve teeth of green bronze, each the shape of a small bell, laid out in a row by someone who has rung every one. Beside them a sack with a tin tag, in a factor's hand: NORTH.".
+  Topic halm_tray_ask with halm "Ask Halm where the bell-teeth on the cloth came from" when exchangeLot() once {
+    Says "'A cart,' says Halm, without looking round from the tray. 'Last week, from a Founders' Exchange in the north, a factor who buys by the pound and sells to the Collegium by the ring. They always have the stuff that rings.' He picks up a tooth and flicks it; the note goes on and on. 'Twelve. Each a voice, each pitched to the next. They come out of a wheel, and a wheel with twelve teeth missing is a wheel that has stopped doing something.' He puts it down very carefully. 'I paid double. I paid for it. I'd like, if you have any idea where the wheel is, to have it back where it belongs, and to have never been paid for it.'".
+  }
 }
